  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Space Exploration Technologies has $2.2B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date, across 66 awards with an average award size of $33M. Obligations fell 27.3% year over year. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer with $2B in obligations, followed by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $156M. Market exposure is concentrated: Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation accounts for $1.5B, Satellite Telecommunications accounts for $417M, and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences accounts for $250M. Historical annual obligations rose through FY24 and have pulled back since: FY21 was $1.5B, FY22 was $2.1B, FY23 was $2.4B, FY24 was $3.4B, FY25 was $3B, and FY26 is $2.2B. The current-year snapshot shows a handful of very large awards and one primary buyer driving the majority of obligations, while several other agencies and NAICS categories register much smaller totals.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the top agency buyers?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense leads with $2B in obligations for FY2026 to date. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration follows with $156M. Other named agencies include the Department of State at $3.1M and the Department of Justice at $600K. The Department of Transportation and Department of the Interior appear with de minimis amounts of $9K and $219 respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which markets capture the largest share of obligations?",
        "body": "Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation is the largest market with $1.5B in obligations. Satellite Telecommunications follows with $417M, and the R&D category in the physical, engineering, and life sciences totals $250M. Other listed markets include Radio and Television Broadcasting and Wireless Communications Equipment Manufacturing at $120K and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $0."
      }
